{"from": "passage:jn-16-02", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:jn-16-02-persecution"}
{"from": "passage:jn-16-03", "type": "asserts", "to": "claim:jn-16-02-persecution"}
{"from": "claim:jn-16-02-persecution", "type": "mentions", "to": "person:jesus-of-nazareth"}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-199-sanhedrin", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-16-02-persecution", "grade": "C", "note": "Ant. 20.9.1 (200-203): the high priest Ananus 'assembled the sanhedrim of judges, and brought before them the brother of Jesus, who was called Christ, whose name was James, and some others... and delivered them to be stoned' (62 CE) - Jesus' followers killed by the Jewish authorities in the period. It attests the event-type, never the prediction; Ananus judged the law broken, matching 'will think he offers service to God' only as a parallel."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-annals-15-44", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-16-02-persecution", "grade": "C", "note": "Annals 15.44: under Nero, Christians were arrested, convicted 'not so much on the count of arson as for hatred of the human race', and executed in great numbers (64 CE) - the killing of Jesus' followers as an event in the following decades, from a hostile Roman angle. Context for the event-type, not the prediction."}
{"from": "text:birkat-ha-minim", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-16-02-persecution", "grade": "D", "note": "The Twelfth Benediction curses the heretics (minim) and is widely linked to the expulsion John describes, but its date is disputed (Yavneh to c. 135 CE hypotheses) and the fetched article states no good evidence survives of its use against minim at this time and no herem before the 3rd century; the identification with John 16:2's expulsion is Martyn's disputed thesis. Dispute named; grade D."}
{"from": "text:tacitus-annals-15-44", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-tacitus-annals-15"}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-199-sanhedrin", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-ant20"}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-20-199-sanhedrin", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikisource-ant20-consent"}
{"from": "text:birkat-ha-minim", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-birkat-haminim"}
{"from": "text:birkat-ha-minim", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-birkat-martyn"}
{"from": "text:birkat-ha-minim", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-birkat-no-ban"}
{"from": "text:birkat-ha-minim", "type": "documented_by", "to": "source:wikipedia-birkat-no-herem"}
{"from": "claim:jn-16-02-persecution", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:jn-09-22-aposynagogos", "note": "Difference: John 9:22 records an agreed rule - expulsion for anyone who confessed Jesus as the Christ; 16:2 generalizes the expulsion to the disciples themselves and adds killing. Same aposynagogos material, different scope (Birkat ha-Minim serves both at the same disputed grade D)."}
{"from": "claim:jn-16-02-persecution", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-10-17-councils-synagogues", "note": "Difference: Matthew 10:17 predicts flogging of disciples in the synagogues before councils; John 16:2 predicts expulsion from the synagogues and killing. Same setting (synagogue disciplinary action against disciples), different measure."}
{"from": "passage:jn-16-32", "type": "parallels", "to": "passage:mt-26-31", "note": "The same scattering prediction: Matthew 26:31 quotes Zechariah 13:7 ('strike the shepherd, and the sheep scattered') and sets it 'this night'; John 16:32 gives the prediction without the quotation and adds 'you will leave me alone'."}
